---
// Lesson 10.2f — Card Jam: Showcase
// rpg jam: card game showcase; jam season 1 ends; bridge to Unit 11.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.2f — Card Jam: Showcase</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Card games hit different in an arcade row: you can watch a whole
    match over someone's shoulder, and the READ of a card game —
    the little math in the eyes — is visible. Jam season one ends
    today; the Create PT begins in three B days
    (<a href="11.1a.html">11.1a</a>). Play like it matters, because
    it does: your Create PT could be this game's descendant.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Showcase</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The arcade row, card edition.</strong> Same contract
    as <a href="10.1f.html">10.1f</a>: host and player in equal
    measure. Watch specifically for the card-game-only moments:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The <strong>hesitation hover</strong> — the tester
            holds a card and reads it twice. Was it confusion or
            calculation? Your notes say which (confusion =
            design debt; calculation = design WINNING)</li>
        <li>The <strong>rule question</strong> — anything you have
            to answer out loud is tomorrow's on-screen hint
            text</li>
        <li>The <strong>rematch demand</strong> — the card game's
            highest compliment. Log which games got one</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Star votes:</strong> best rule set, best card art
    (your <a href="10.2b.html">10.2b</a> assets), best AI
    personality (<a href="10.2d.html">10.2d</a> — could you TELL
    what the AI wanted?).</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Retro + Bridge</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Retro (same four quadrants, <a href="10.1f.html">10.1f</a>
    format) plus ONE card-jam-only question:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>What did LISTS let your game do that single variables
            never could? (Name the exact moment in your game
            that is ONLY possible because of a list.)</li>
        <li>Was the starter's gift — the empty methods — a good
            learning design, or would building from zero have
            taught more? (This is an <a href="9.10.html">9.10</a>
            fork-ethics question in disguise.)</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>The bridge to the Create PT — read this twice:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>The Create PT (<a href="../exam/create-performance-task.html">full
            guide</a>) requires: a list, a procedure with a
            parameter, an algorithm with sequencing + selection +
            iteration, a call, input → output. Your card game HAS
            ALL SIX ALREADY — shuffle (list + iteration +
            selection), AI_Turn (algorithm + procedure), Deal
            (procedure + call), the score line (selection).
            Read the requirements against your own code and
            CHECK THEM OFF, out loud, today.</li>
        <li>You may even PORT this jam's code as your PT starting
            point — the rules allow work you wrote in class.
            Fork yourself: <a href="11.1a.html">11.1a</a> is
            three B days away, and it is not a new beginning;
            it is a continuation.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Archive + tag</h3>
        <ol>
            <li><code>git tag jam-card &amp;&amp; git push --tags</code>;
                RETRO.md update with star counts + rematch
                demand count.</li>
            <li>Write the 3-sentence PT candidate pitch: "As my
                Create PT I could ___ because it already has
                ___ and I would add ___." Bring it to
                <a href="11.1a.html">11.1a</a>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Reflect: your own code, read cold</h3>
        <p>
            Which requirement from the Create PT checklist was easiest
            to point at in your card jam code, and which one would you
            still have to explain carefully to a stranger — and why
            that difference?
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Host + play in the showcase, collecting hover/rule/rematch data</li>
            <li>Name the moment in your game only a list makes possible</li>
            <li>Check off all six Create PT requirements against your own jam code</li>
            <li>Arrive at the PT launch with a candidate pitch written</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — the jam archive</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/create-performance-task.html">Create Performance Task</a> — what begins next</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next unit: <a href="11.1a.html">11.1a — Create PT Launch: Pick Your Program</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="10.2f" title="10.2f Card Jam: Showcase — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
